comments.body:
@Luap99, you're right, Unix sockets don't have TCP RSTs and the server does flush correctly. 

I looked it up a little more. The actual race condition might be on the client side. When the server calls a full `Close()` immediately after writing, it abruptly destroys the socket. This races with Go's client-side `bufio.Reader` (which wraps the `101` response body), causing it to error out before draining the final buffered chunk.

I wanted to verify before making any changes to the pr. I was thinking whether using a half-close (`CloseWrite` / `shutdown(SHUT_WR)`) might just fix this. It sends a clean `EOF` to the client, keeping the receive path open just long enough for the client to read the final bytes.
